    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you compare the thermal conductivity and heat dissipation rates of ceramic vs semi-metallic brake pads during heavy braking cycles? specifically looking at how they handle heat soak in high-performance applications.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads generally exhibit lower thermal conductivity and heat dissipation rates compared to semi-metallic variants, which can lead to more rapid localized heat buildup during heavy braking. Under sustained high-performance conditions, ceramic pads often have limited capacity to rapidly release heat, increasing the risk of brake fade due to thermal soak, whereas semi-metallic pads transfer heat away from the friction surface more aggressively. Consequently, while ceramic pads offer better fade resistance under specific fed ducting systems, semi-metallic composition is typically superior for managing short-to-moderate intense braking cycles where rapid dissipation is critical.",
      "source": "target",
