    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under extreme thermal shock, ceramic brake pads are highly resistant to cracking but frequently suffer from \"glazing,\" where the coating melts and融入到 the rotor surface, leading to increased wear and potential brake drop-off. Semi-metallic pads, lacking a stable abrasive coating, are prone to rapid wear and uneven pad deterioration as soft metals fuse to the rotor upon sudden cooling, causing disc leathers. This thermal cycling can also trigger erratic caliper behavior through warp-induced drag, as assembled dryers expand and contract at different rates than the boot assembly, stiffening the movement in ceramic systems. Ultimately, both materials risk locking up the calipers if moisture is trapped and rapidly flash-boiled by the intense heat.",
